-- Derived from wecon_wtest/WECON-02.DATA, with the WECON/WTEST economic-limit
-- testing removed and the following changes, so that the drawdown limit rather
-- than well shut-in is what the case measures:
--
--   * a skin of 20 on both producers, lowering the productivity index from
--     ~360 to ~75 sm3/day/bar so that the drawdown at the rate targets lands
--     in a realistic 5-30 bar range rather than 1-3 bar;
--   * a second water injector 'E-2H' in the western fault compartment, which
--     in WECON-02 has no pressure support at all;
--   * both injectors put on a 300 bar BHP limit so they self-regulate to the
--     voidage the producers need.  Without this the producers deplete to the
--     75 bar BHP limit and shut, and a shut well cannot demonstrate a
--     drawdown limit.
--
-- WELDRAW-BASE and WELDRAW-01 are identical apart from the WELDRAW keyword, so
-- their summary vectors can be compared step for step.

-- WELDRAW-01 applies the drawdown limit.  Compare against the WELDRAW-BASE base
-- case, which is the same model with the WELDRAW keyword removed.
--
-- The schedule has three regimes so the effect is visible as a step change:
--
--   10 Oct 2018 - 1 Dec 2018   no WELDRAW yet; identical to WELDRAW-BASE.
--   1 Dec 2018 - 1 Mar 2019    WELDRAW active: 5.0 bar on D-1H, 10.0 bar on
--                              D-2H.  Both limits are below the natural
--                              drawdown, so both wells are cut back.
--   1 Mar 2019 - 1 Jun 2019    the limit on D-1H is removed by defaulting
--                              item 2, and D-2H is tightened to 6.0 bar.
--
-- Item 4 (USE_LIMIT) and item 5 (GRID_BLOCKS) are left at NO / AVG, the only
-- combination OPM Flow currently supports.

--
-- To verify the drawdown limit from the summary output: WMCTL reports 12
-- (drawdown control) while the limit is what holds the well back, and the
-- rate divided by the productivity index, WLPR / WPIL for a liquid target
-- phase or WGPR / WPIG for a gas one, reproduces the drawdown limit exactly,
-- since the limit enters as Qmax = Dmax * sum_j(Tw_j * M_j).  That ratio is
-- the exact check.
--
-- WUDD, defined below as WBP - WBHP, tracks the drawdown closely but is not
-- the same quantity that the limit is applied to: with the default WPAVE it
-- weights the connections by their transmissibility factor alone, whereas
-- the AVG option of WELDRAW weights them by transmissibility times mobility.
-- The two coincide when the mobility is the same in every connection.  In
-- these cases the difference stays below 0.02 bar for the liquid target
-- phase and below 0.1 bar for the gas one, so WUDD is a good diagnostic,
-- but small departures from the limit are expected and are not failures.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUNSPEC
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DIMENS
 6 5 7 /

OIL
WATER

--
GAS
DISGAS
VAPOIL

METRIC

START
 10 'OCT' 2018 /

--
GRIDOPTS
'YES'		0 /


EQLDIMS
 1 1*  25 /


--
REGDIMS
-- max. ntfip  nmfipr  max. nrfreg   max. ntfreg
    2          1       1*            0    /

--
--
WELLDIMS
--max.well  max.con/well  max.grup  max.w/grup
  4        4            10        10   /

--
TABDIMS
--ntsfun     ntpvt  max.nssfun  max.nppvt  max.ntfip  max.nrpvt
  1          1      50          60          2         60 /

-- Dimensions for the WUDD drawdown UDQ defined in the SCHEDULE section.
UDQDIMS
 12 12 2 2 2 2 0 2 /


--
UNIFIN
UNIFOUT

--

